## Empirical finding (F5, 2026-06-13): QEMU mcycle/minstret are host timers

On `andes_ae350`, QEMU's `mcycle`/`minstret` CSRs read back a host-derived
free-running value (~1.3e15, mcycle ~= minstret, drifting ~600k per
instruction), NOT the architectural retired-instruction count. The plugin
drains faithfully; the value is just not a per-instruction counter. So:
- Record alignment is by ATTEMPT INDEX (the k-th drain on each side), never
  by minstret value. The original "align by minstret" idea is dead on
  arrival for the QEMU oracle.
- minstret/mcycle are EXCLUDED from the lock-step diff (already excluded by
  `diff_states`). vsim's minstret IS clean (== record index under
  stopcount), so the records still carry it for human inspection.
- "Trap CSRs compared" (PM decision 1) is honored selectively: mepc + mcause
  are well-defined and reveal trap-handling bugs, so the lock-step diff adds
  them. mtval (implementation-defined for illegal insns) and mstatus
  (dirty-bit differences) are captured in the records but NOT gated, to
  avoid false positives. This matches industry tandem-sim practice
  (compare rd/pc/trap-cause, not full CSR state).

## Risks

- dcsr.step support is per-RTL (empirical, like NDS_NUM_PRIVILEGE_LEVELS).
  F2 verifies the write; unsupported engines get a clear abort (lock-step)
  or the bracket fallback (refine).
- minstret/mcycle incrementing in debug mode inflates alignment; F2 sets
  dcsr.stopcount if implemented; the F6 trap fixture is the gate.
- Fetch-faulting instructions may not get a QEMU exec callback at all
  (translation fails before the tb is built). If observed, the record
  streams skew by one at that point; the diff still fails loudly there,
  but the index attribution may point at the neighbor. Note in docs;
  revisit only if a real testbench hits it.
- Lock-step throughput (~tens of steps/s) caps practical testbench size at
  ~10-50k attempts. Mitigation: coarse haltreq pass first
  (--cosim-interval), then refine -- same loop as F7 but from boot.
- Async (F8): deterministic injection at an EXACT attempt index likely
  needs a QEMU core patch -- the plugin API only observes, it cannot raise
  an interrupt or force the check point. Scope the hook narrowly (set mip +
  take at the next insn boundary), reusing the fork-patch precedent.
- Async (F9): the mcause-MSB detector can miss back-to-back interrupts of
  the SAME cause if mcause/mepc do not change between them. The rt_irq_timer
  gate covers the single-interrupt case first; revisit nested/same-cause
  trains only if a real testbench needs them.

